+
+## JSON Patch (RFC 6902)
+
+```ts
+import { applyPatch } from "./src/index.js";
+
+applyPatch({ foo: "bar" }, [
+  { op: "add", path: "/baz", value: "qux" },
+  { op: "test", path: "/foo", value: "bar" },
+]); // { foo: "bar", baz: "qux" }
+```
+
+`applyPatch(document, patch)` applies the operations `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy` and `test` in
+order, with paths as JSON Pointers (RFC 6901), and returns the result as a new value. Neither argument is modified,
+and the result shares no structure with them. It throws `JsonPatchInvalidError` when the patch is not valid (not an
+array, bad `op`, missing or invalid `path`, `value` or `from`) and `JsonPatchOperationError` when an operation fails
+(missing target, bad array index, `move` into its own child, failed `test`, removing the whole document). Both extend
+`JsonPatchError`, whose `index` is the failing operation's position. Per RFC 6902 §5 a failing patch has no effect.
